WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.170
Thanks to Babbel for sponsoring this episode
of Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:10.306
Click the link in the description to start
learning a new language today with Babbel.

00:00:14.338 --> 00:00:19.939
You might climb up a tree to get a bird’s
eye view of the world, or to pick some fruit.

00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:27.429
But for our master of microscopes, James,
trees are a way to get a very close up view

00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:32.040
of something much smaller than an apple.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.730
Yeah, it’s time to talk about tardigrades.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:42.120
Along with all the squirrels and birds and
bugs scurrying up and down and around trees,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:49.220
there are also tiny little tree tardigrades
tucked into the watery drops inside moss and

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.530
lichen that grow on the tree’s surface.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:57.560
They’re not actually called tree tardigrades,
they’re called terrestrial tardigrades,

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:02.329
because they live on land and they’re a
little bit different from the tardigrades

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:04.589
we’ve shown here thus far.

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:10.359
Those have been from freshwater sources like
ponds and rivers, and they’re most likely

00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:13.159
of the genus Pseudobiotus.

00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:15.520
And why do we feature them so much?

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.610
Well, it’s certainly not because they’re
easier to find.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.189
In fact, we have a harder time tracking down
the freshwater species than the terrestrial

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:25.189
ones.

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:30.369
James knows exactly where to find terrestrial
tardigrades: his front yard.

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:34.749
They live in some of the old trees there,
and when it rains, James will scratch some

00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:41.119
patches of moss off them and then bring them
inside to observe the tardigrades hidden in

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:42.860
their depths.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.170
So why the freshwater bias?

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:52.000
We’ve got two answers for you, one is scientific,
one .... is not.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.820
On the science side, terrestrial tardigrades
may be easier for us to find and gather, but

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.069
moss samples can be tricky to work with.

00:02:01.069 --> 00:02:07.420
They contain lots of remains from other animals
that live in trees along with tons of bacteria,

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.880
which divide really fast and consume all the
oxygen on the slide, suffocating other life

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.930
like our water bears.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:19.730
We don’t have this same challenge with their
freshwater counterparts, so it’s easy for

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:24.560
us to keep them alive in our slides to watch
for months.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.210
And that is why we’ve been able to document
everything from their mating habits to their

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:34.790
hatching eggs to their food—it’s like
our own little tardigrade reality TV show.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:41.560
But we also said there was a less scientific
explanation for our Pseudobiotus bias.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.910
And we don’t want to hurt any feelings here,
but really it’s just this: We like them.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.660
They’re just very cute.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:54.430
They’ve got those wiggly legs and the beautiful
black eyes, and somehow they just stand out

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.850
even from other tardigrades.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:04.070
But we need to set aside both these scientific
and superficial excuses because terrestrial

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.870
tardigrades are pretty cool.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:12.380
The ones we find usually belong to one of
two genera, both of which you can see here.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.570
That reddish one is in the genus Ramazottius.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.330
And the other one is Milnesium.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:24.710
The name of the Ramazottius genus comes from
Giuseppe Ramazzotti, who was a tardigradologist,

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:29.080
which—yes—is the actual name of an actual
job.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.721
And their reddish color comes from pigments
in their cuticle, which is, basically, their

00:03:33.721 --> 00:03:35.170
exoskeleton.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:40.927
We think this color might be a way to protect
the inside of the tardigrade from the sun’s

00:03:40.927 --> 00:03:45.560
radiation...basically, tardigrade sunscreen!

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.430
Whether or not that is true, we can certainly
appreciate how the colors make it look like

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:55.250
a ruddy, half-translucent alligator with way
too many legs.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:59.790
While we’ve talked about tardigrades quite
a bit on this channel, one of their weird

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:04.330
quirks that we haven’t gotten into are their
coelomocytes.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:11.860
See, tardigrades are multicellular organisms,
but they don’t have a circulatory or respiratory

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:12.860
system.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:19.090
So, how do they deliver all of the things
they need to deliver to all of their cells?

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:21.079
Well, they have coelomocytes.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:28.160
These are little storage cells that hold sugars
and proteins and lipids, and they transport

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.580
them around the tardigrade’s body.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:36.350
It’s like little presents delivered from
cell to cell in a bubble that floats around

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:37.360
the organism.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.660
It’s kind of adorable.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.520
In some tardigrades, the number of storage
cells stays constant.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.350
But in others, like our Milnesium tardigrades,
the number of storage cells will go down when

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:55.440
the tardigrade is dealing with starvation,
or when they go through anhydrobiosis, that

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.650
famous state where the tardigrades dry themselves
out to survive.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:05.650
The milnesium tardigrade may not have the
color of the Ramazottius, but it is still

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.440
very fun to watch.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.170
Here you can see its little legs at work as
the tardigrade ambles across the slide.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:17.400
It’s like watching your cat’s paws from
underneath a glass table, but instead of toe

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.950
beans, you got micro claws.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:26.030
And those claws are one of the morphological
characteristics that help distinguish between

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.530
different species of Milnesium.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:35.889
Indeed, we can even see these claws in an
extinct ancient species, Milnesium swolenskyi

00:05:35.889 --> 00:05:43.580
And yeah, it is weird that we know about an
ancient tardigrade species and that we can

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:47.400
tell it’s in the milnesium genus from it’s
little claws.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.630
How?

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:56.740
Well, someone, and thank goodness they did,
looked very closely at an 89.3 million year

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:04.730
old glob of amber dug up in New Jersey and
found, inside, the only known example of milnesium

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:10.960
swolensky...complete with tiny microclaws.

00:06:13.124 --> 00:06:18.730
Milnesium tardigrades are carnivorous...even
their larvae are able to feed on rotifers

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:22.970
soon after hatching—though they usually
aren’t able to eat a whole one until they

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.860
grow a bit.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:29.930
Eventually, as they mature, they’re able
to swallow a rotifer whole.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:35.820
As we mentioned before, terrestrial tardigrades
go through anhydrobiosis, which translates

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:37.400
to “life without water”.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:42.120
And this is not just a quirk of tardigrade
existence.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:47.110
This is how they survive on land.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:50.960
Life in moss is a life of extremes.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.540
When there’s water, things are great.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:07:01.580
But when the moss dries out, which it does,
the tardigrades inside it must somehow survive.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:10.560
With no water surrounding them, their bodies,
when this happens, can drop to 1-3% of the

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.780
water content it had before.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:19.940
And that tardigrade will shrink, like this
one did, and turn into what’s called a tun.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:27.440
It will remain metabolically at rest, famously
capable of withstanding all sorts of extremes,

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:33.390
until water returns to its surroundings and
fills the tardigrade up with life again.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:38.410
The main thing we know about tardigrades is
that they enter this state which allows them

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:44.790
to survive seemingly indefinitely and deal
with all kinds of extreme environments.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:51.832
And how these tardigrades enter and survive
the tun state is complex and mysterious, and

00:07:51.832 --> 00:07:58.008
different species are able to withstand more
extremes than others.

00:08:02.563 --> 00:08:07.620
Another thing I don’t think we talk enough
about when talking about tardigrades, is that

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:12.979
there are likely upwards of 1000 species of
tardigrade!

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:18.669
For comparison, there are 6500 mammals...total.

00:08:18.669 --> 00:08:20.210
ALL MAMMALS.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:21.470
6,500.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:25.160
Tardigrades, over 1,000 all by themselves.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:32.520
So, you gotta wonder, in the face of all of
these tardigrade species, how do scientists

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.640
even pick which ones to study?

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:40.099
Well, much like how we’ve got our reasons
for the tardigrades we focus on here, they

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:41.550
have theirs.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:46.250
One group of scientists picked Ramazzottius
varieornatus because it’s one of the most

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:53.300
stress-tolerant species, an extreme of extremes
that helped the researchers find a tardigrade-unique

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:56.590
protein that protects their DNA from damage.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:09:00.270
And sometimes, it’s just a matter of logistics.

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:05.440
Astrobiologists turn to extremophiles like
tardigrades because they might give us clues

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.870
into what multicellular life in extreme environments
might look like.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:16.730
But Earthly scientists are still subject to
Earthly constraints, like time and other projects

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:19.000
and life away from experiments.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:25.530
And so sometimes, logistics are the most important
thing, which is why one group put forth the

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:30.400
argument that while the Milnesium tardigrade
might have a lot of the wondrous survival

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:36.380
capabilities they want to study, they’re
also just a pain to take care of.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:41.940
Their carnivorous diets mean they need to
be constantly fed, and they just need a lot

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:43.270
of care.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:49.940
But—the scientists pointed out—the Ramazzottius
varieornatus can survive just on algae and

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:57.000
requires a lot less immediate care, making
them much easier to manage as a research organism.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:01.660
Some might call this laziness, we call it
good planning.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:06.450
You don’t want your entire experiment ruined
because somebody can’t come into the lab

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:10.890
to toss a bunch of rotifers in the Milnesium
tank.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:16.300
Every journey comes down to choices, but we’re
fortunate that any choice we make with the

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:25.030
microcosmos always seems to lead somewhere
fascinating.

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:30.200
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:31.200
us.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.040
Here’s a quick German lesson for you.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:41.750
